Make nvmath.device importable without numba

On this ref the numba-cuda backend is not optional: device/__init__.py imports
cublasdx unconditionally, which pulls common_opaque_tensor -> common_numba ->
{llvm_array, vector_types_numba}, and cusolverdx pulls cusolverdx_backend. Those
modules import numba, numba.cuda and llvmlite at module scope, so
`import nvmath.device` fails outright unless numba-cuda is installed - the
_HAS_NUMBA gate below them never gets a chance to run.

This CI job exercises only the numba-cuda-mlir backend, so move the numba-only
code off the unconditional import path:

  * split common_opaque_tensor into a numba-free half (OpaqueLayout,
    _LIBMATHDX_RUNTIME - both pure Python/numpy, and all cublasdx needs) and a
    new common_opaque_tensor_numba holding the type/model registrations;
  * import the new module from the existing `if _HAS_NUMBA:` block and repoint
    cublasdx_numba at it;
  * make cusolverdx_backend's `from numba import cuda` lazy by splitting out
    get_universal_fatbin_data(), which returns the raw bytes, and have the
    numba-cuda-mlir backend use that instead of unwrapping numba's Fatbin.

Applied by ci/tools/run-nvmath-python-mlir-tests.

